I"�<h2 id="disk-databases">Disk databases</h2>

<p>Astrophysical objects are often spheres or disks, and some of the more close-to-human-scale disks are those that form (protoplanetary disks) or are found in (debris disks) planetary systems. Come and have some fun exploring these disks below.</p>

<p>These databases are inspired by Karl Stapelfeldt’s <a href="https://www.circumstellardisks.org">Catalog of Circumstellar Disks</a>, Nicole Pawellek’s <a href="https://www.physik.uni-jena.de/21956/catalog-of-resolved-debris-disks">database of resolved debris disks</a> and the <a href="https://exoplanet.eu/catalog/">Catalogue of Exoplanets</a>. They were made with the help of Claude, which I recently got access to from Caltech.</p>

<p>If you’re doing some serious science with these, be very careful. It might be a good idea to check with me where these things come from.</p>

<h3 id="debris-disk-explorer"><a href="https://yinuohan.github.io/debris-explorer/">Debris disk explorer</a></h3>

<p>All the resolved debris disks. Come and check out some cousins of our Kuiper belt.</p>

<h3 id="ysos-and-protoplanetary-disk-explorer"><a href="https://yinuohan.github.io/yso-explorer/">YSOs and protoplanetary disk explorer</a></h3>

<p>All the resolved planet-forming disks. What planets and debris disks will they form? Only time (10 million years) will tell (about an order of magnitude longer than the time since the emergence of early modern humans, but more than an order magnitude faster than cycle of plate tectonics).</p>
